        "type": "tekst",
        "titel": "1.7 Sociologists look to the future",
        "toetsstof": true,
        "tekst": "The earliest sociologists lived through the French and Industrial Revolutions, and they **reacted differently** to the new order, just as sociologists respond differently to society today.\n\n- **Comte** and later **Toennies** feared that people would be uprooted from long-established communities and overpowered by change. Comte's response was conservative: shore up the family and traditional morality.\n- **Karl Marx (1818-83)** worried little about the loss of tradition, but could not condone the way industrial technology concentrated great wealth in the hands of a small elite while so many faced hunger and misery.\n\nRadically different prescriptions, but a shared conviction: **society rests on much more than individual choice**, and individual lives are framed by the broader society.\n\n**Continuing change.** The changes of the twenty-first century are as momentous as those of the nineteenth: digital technologies, new media, new reproductive technologies, new global connections. Traditional families, religions, patterns of work and government are all being rethought. People are given no clear blueprint for how to live, so they have to ask what kind of life they want to lead: they become **more individualised, less committed to common standards, more prone to self-reflection**. Divisions of class, ethnicity, age and gender are more noticeable, and conflicts over religion and culture are rife. Just as sociology was born of the Industrial Revolution, it now faces the challenge of what the book calls the **postmodern or information society**.\n\n**Sociology does not stand alone.** A box in the chapter places it next to its neighbours: **history** (all social phenomena have histories: when did this happen and what led to it?), **philosophy** (the assumptions about human nature, freedom and knowledge behind any theory), **anthropology** (how social things are organised across different cultures, and the corrective against **ethnocentrism**, judging another culture by the standard of your own), and **literature** (novels, film and art as visions of human life). Newer fields such as women's and gender studies, media and cultural studies, ethnic studies, human rights and queer studies are usually more interdisciplinary and focus on one issue."
